+ 8 = 8.\n\\]\nTherefore, the rightmost non-zero digit of the expression is 8, and there are no trailing zeros before this digit. Thus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\((8, 0)\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(8, 0)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(8, 0)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression \\((1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001)\\), we will simplify and analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\nWe can factor out common terms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit of this expression. We will do this by considering the last digits of each term and their product.\n\nFirst, consider the last digit of \\((1001001)(1000100)\\):\n\\[\n1 \\times 0 = 0.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this term is 0.\n\nNext, consider the last digit of \\((989899)(11102)\\):\n\\[\n9 \\times 2 = 18.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this term is 8.\n\nAdding these two results together:\n\\[\n0 + 8 = 8.\n\\]\nTherefore, the rightmost non-zero digit of the expression is 8, and there are no trailing zeros before this digit. Thus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\((8, 0)\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(8, 0)}\n\\]","id":"algebra:117"}
